		</div>

						<div id=3D"siteSub" class=3D"noprint">From Wikipedia, the free encycl=
opedia</div>
					</div>
					<div id=3D"contentSub"><div id=3D"mw-content-subtitle"><span class=3D"=
mw-redirectedfrom">(Redirected from <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/i=
ndex.php?title=3DLinear_feedback_shift_register&amp;redirect=3Dno" class=3D=
"mw-redirect" title=3D"Linear feedback shift register">Linear feedback shif=
t register</a>)</span></div></div>
				=09
				=09
					<div id=3D"mw-content-text" class=3D"mw-body-content"><div class=3D"mw=
-subjectpageheader">
</div><div class=3D"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output" lang=3D"en" dir=3D"ltr=
"><div class=3D"shortdescription nomobile noexcerpt noprint searchaux" styl=
e=3D"display:none">Type of shift register in computing</div>
<div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">"LFSR" redir=
ects here. For the airport using that ICAO code, see <a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Reims_%E2%80%93_Champagne_Air_Base" title=3D"Reims =E2=
=80=93 Champagne Air Base">Reims =E2=80=93 Champagne Air Base</a>.</div>
<p class=3D"mw-empty-elt">
</p>
<table class=3D"box-More_citations_needed plainlinks metadata ambox ambox-c=
ontent ambox-Refimprove" role=3D"presentation"><tbody><tr><td class=3D"mbox=
-image"><div class=3D"mbox-image-div"><span typeof=3D"mw:File"><a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Question_book-new.svg" class=3D"mw-file-d=
escription"><img alt=3D"icon" src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia=
/en/thumb/9/99/Question_book-new.svg/60px-Question_book-new.svg.png" decodi=
ng=3D"async" width=3D"50" height=3D"39" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-file=
-width=3D"512" data-file-height=3D"399"></a></span></div></td><td class=3D"=
mbox-text"><div class=3D"mbox-text-span">This article <b>needs additional c=
itations for <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Verifiabili=
ty" title=3D"Wikipedia:Verifiability">verification</a></b>.<span class=3D"h=
ide-when-compact"> Please help <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spe=
cial:EditPage/Linear-feedback_shift_register" title=3D"Special:EditPage/Lin=
ear-feedback shift register">improve this article</a> by <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Referencing_for_beginners" title=3D"Help:Refere=
ncing for beginners">adding citations to reliable sources</a>. Unsourced ma=
terial may be challenged and removed.<br><small><span class=3D"plainlinks">=
<i>Find sources:</i>&nbsp;<a rel=3D"nofollow" class=3D"external text" href=
=3D"https://www.google.com/search?as_eq=3Dwikipedia&amp;q=3D%22Linear-feedb=
ack+shift+register%22">"Linear-feedback shift register"</a>&nbsp;=E2=80=93&=
nbsp;<a rel=3D"nofollow" class=3D"external text" href=3D"https://www.google=
.com/search?tbm=3Dnws&amp;q=3D%22Linear-feedback+shift+register%22+-wikiped=
ia&amp;tbs=3Dar:1">news</a>&nbsp;<b>=C2=B7</b> <a rel=3D"nofollow" class=3D=
"external text" href=3D"https://www.google.com/search?&amp;q=3D%22Linear-fe=
edback+shift+register%22&amp;tbs=3Dbkt:s&amp;tbm=3Dbks">newspapers</a>&nbsp=
;<b>=C2=B7</b> <a rel=3D"nofollow" class=3D"external text" href=3D"https://=
www.google.com/search?tbs=3Dbks:1&amp;q=3D%22Linear-feedback+shift+register=
%22+-wikipedia">books</a>&nbsp;<b>=C2=B7</b> <a rel=3D"nofollow" class=3D"e=
xternal text" href=3D"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=3D%22Linear-feed=
back+shift+register%22">scholar</a>&nbsp;<b>=C2=B7</b> <a rel=3D"nofollow" =
class=3D"external text" href=3D"https://www.jstor.org/action/doBasicSearch?=
Query=3D%22Linear-feedback+shift+register%22&amp;acc=3Don&amp;wc=3Don">JSTO=
R</a></span></small></span>  <span class=3D"date-container"><i>(<span class=
=3D"date">March 2009</span>)</i></span><span class=3D"hide-when-compact"><i=
> (<small><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Maintenance_templat=
e_removal" title=3D"Help:Maintenance template removal">Learn how and when t=
o remove this message</a></small>)</i></span></div></td></tr></tbody></tabl=
e>
<p>In <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computing" title=3D"Computin=
g">computing</a>, a <b>linear-feedback shift register</b> (<b>LFSR</b>) is =
a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shift_register" title=3D"Shift r=
egister">shift register</a> whose input bit is a <a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Linear#Boolean_functions" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Lin=
ear">linear function</a> of its previous state.
</p><p>The most commonly used linear function of single bits is <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exclusive-or" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"=
Exclusive-or">exclusive-or</a> (XOR). Thus, an LFSR is most often a shift r=
egister whose input bit is driven by the XOR of some bits of the overall sh=
ift register value.
</p><p>The initial value of the LFSR is called the seed, and because the op=
eration of the register is deterministic, the stream of values produced by =
the register is completely determined by its current (or previous) state. L=
ikewise, because the register has a finite number of possible states, it mu=
st eventually enter a repeating cycle. However, an LFSR with a <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primitive_polynomial_(field_theory)" title=3D"=
Primitive polynomial (field theory)">well-chosen feedback function</a> can =
produce a sequence of bits that appears random and has a <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maximal_length_sequence" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=
=3D"Maximal length sequence">very long cycle</a>.
</p><p>Applications of LFSRs include generating <a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Pseudorandomness" title=3D"Pseudorandomness">pseudo-random nu=
mbers</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pseudorandom_noise" tit=
le=3D"Pseudorandom noise">pseudo-noise sequences</a>, fast digital counters=
, and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitening_sequences" class=
=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Whitening sequences">whitening sequences</a>. Bot=
h hardware and software implementations of LFSRs are common.
</p><p>The mathematics of a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyclic=
_redundancy_check" title=3D"Cyclic redundancy check">cyclic redundancy chec=
k</a>, used to provide a quick check against transmission errors, are close=
ly related to those of an LFSR.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-1" class=3D"reference"><=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear-feedback_shift_register#cite=
_note-1"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>1<span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>]</span></a></sup> In general, the arithmetics behind LFSRs makes them ver=
y elegant as an object to study and implement. One can produce relatively c=
omplex logics with simple building blocks. However, other methods, that are=
 less elegant but perform better, should be considered as well.
</p>
<meta property=3D"mw:PageProp/toc">
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Fibonacci_LFSRs">Fibonacci =
LFSRs</h2><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-brac=
ket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DLinear=
-feedback_shift_register&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D1" title=3D"Edit s=
ection: Fibonacci LFSRs"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection=
-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class=3D"mw-halign-right" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:LFSR-F16.svg" class=3D"mw-file-description"><=
img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/LFSR-F=
16.svg/500px-LFSR-F16.svg.png" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"351" height=3D"1=
21" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-file-width=3D"351" data-file-height=3D"1=
21"></a><figcaption>A 16-bit <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fibon=
acci" title=3D"Fibonacci">Fibonacci</a> LFSR. The feedback tap numbers show=
n correspond to a primitive polynomial in the table, so the register cycles=
 through the maximum number of 65535 states excluding the all-zeroes state.=
 The state shown, 0xACE1 (<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hexadeci=
mal" title=3D"Hexadecimal">hexadecimal</a>) will be followed by 0x5670.</fi=
gcaption></figure>
<figure typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><span><span class=3D"mw-tmh-player video">=
<video id=3D"mwe_player_0_placeholder" poster=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.o=
rg/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Fibonacci_linear_feedback_shift_register_%2=
831_bit%29.webm/500px--Fibonacci_linear_feedback_shift_register_%2831_bit%2=
9.webm.jpg" preload=3D"none" data-mw-tmh=3D"" class=3D"mw-file-element" wid=
th=3D"400" height=3D"225" data-durationhint=3D"31" data-mwtitle=3D"Fibonacc=
i_linear_feedback_shift_register_(31_bit).webm" data-mwprovider=3D"wikimedi=
acommons" resource=3D"/wiki/File:Fibonacci_linear_feedback_shift_register_(=
31_bit).webm" playsinline=3D"" disabled=3D"disabled" tabindex=3D"-1" style=
=3D"aspect-ratio: 400 / 225;"></video><a class=3D"mw-tmh-play" href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Fibonacci_linear_feedback_shift_register_(31=
_bit).webm" title=3D"Play video" role=3D"button"><span class=3D"mw-tmh-play=
-icon notheme"></span></a><span class=3D"mw-tmh-duration mw-tmh-label"><spa=
n class=3D"sr-only">Duration: 31 seconds.</span><span aria-hidden=3D"true">=
0:31</span></span></span></span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fi=
le:Fibonacci_linear_feedback_shift_register_(31_bit).webm" class=3D"mw-file=
-magnify"></a><figcaption>A Fibonacci 31 bit linear feedback shift register=
 with taps at positions 28 and 31 (indicated by the yellow LEDs), giving it=
 a maximum cycle and period at this speed of approximately 8 years.</figcap=
tion></figure>
<p>The bit positions that affect the next state are called the <i>taps</i>.=
 In the diagram the taps are [16,14,13,11]. The rightmost bit of the LFSR i=
s called the output bit, which is always also a tap.  To obtain the next st=
ate, the tap bits are XOR-ed sequentially; then, all bits are shifted one p=
lace to the right, with the rightmost bit being discarded, and that result =
of XOR-ing the tap bits is fed back into the now-vacant leftmost bit. To ob=
tain the pseudorandom output stream, read the rightmost bit after each stat=
e transition.
</p>
<ul><li>A maximum-length LFSR produces an <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Maximum_length_sequence" title=3D"Maximum length sequence">m-sequen=
ce</a> (i.e., it cycles through all possible 2<sup><i>m</i></sup>&nbsp;=E2=
=88=92&nbsp;1 states within the shift register except the state where all b=
its are zero), unless it contains all zeros, in which case it will never ch=
ange.</li>
<li>As an alternative to the XOR-based feedback in an LFSR, one can also us=
e <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/XNOR" class=3D"mw-redirect" titl=
e=3D"XNOR">XNOR</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-2" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear-feedback_shift_register#cite_note-2"><s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>2<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></=
a></sup> This function is an <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Affin=
e_transformation" title=3D"Affine transformation">affine map</a>, not stric=
tly a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_map" title=3D"Linear =
map">linear map</a>, but it results in an equivalent polynomial counter who=
se state is the complement of the state of an LFSR. A state with all ones i=
s illegal when using an XNOR feedback, in the same way as a state with all =
zeroes is illegal when using XOR. This state is considered illegal because =
the counter would remain "locked-up" in this state. This method can be adva=
ntageous in hardware LFSRs using flip-flops that start in a zero state, as =
it does not start in a lockup state, meaning that the register does not nee=
d to be seeded in order to begin operation.</li></ul>
<p>The sequence of numbers generated by an LFSR or its XNOR counterpart can=
 be considered a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binary_numeral_sy=
stem" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Binary numeral system">binary numeral =
system</a> just as valid as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gray_c=
ode" title=3D"Gray code">Gray code</a> or the natural binary code.
</p><p>The arrangement of taps for feedback in an LFSR can be expressed in =
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finite_field_arithmetic" title=3D"=
Finite field arithmetic">finite field arithmetic</a> as a <a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial" title=3D"Polynomial">polynomial</a> <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modular_arithmetic" title=3D"Modular =
arithmetic">mod</a> 2. This means that the coefficients of the polynomial m=
ust be 1s or 0s. This is called the feedback polynomial or reciprocal chara=
cteristic polynomial. For example, if the taps are at the 16th, 14th, 13th =
and 11th bits (as shown), the feedback polynomial is
</p>
<dl><dd><span class=3D"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline"><span clas=
s=3D"mwe-math-mathml-inline mwe-math-mathml-a11y" style=3D"display: none;">=
<math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext=3D"{\displaystyl=
e x^{16}+x^{14}+x^{13}+x^{11}+1.}">
  <semantics>
    <mrow class=3D"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
      <mstyle displaystyle=3D"true" scriptlevel=3D"0">
        <msup>
          <mi>x</mi>
          <mrow class=3D"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mn>16</mn>
          </mrow>
        </msup>
        <mo>+</mo>
        <msup>
          <mi>x</mi>
          <mrow class=3D"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mn>14</mn>
          </mrow>
        </msup>
        <mo>+</mo>
        <msup>
          <mi>x</mi>
          <mrow class=3D"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mn>13</mn>
          </mrow>
        </msup>
        <mo>+</mo>
        <msup>
          <mi>x</mi>
          <mrow class=3D"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mn>11</mn>
          </mrow>
        </msup>
        <mo>+</mo>
        <mn>1.</mn>
      </mstyle>
    </mrow>
    <annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">{\displaystyle x^{16}+x^{14}=
+x^{13}+x^{11}+1.}</annotation>
  </semantics>
</math></span><img src=3D"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/rend=
er/svg/e2635c901f8dd49ffa7a57b3b659fbf866972738" class=3D"mwe-math-fallback=
-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert" aria-hidden=3D"true" style=3D"vertical=
-align: -0.505ex; width:25.994ex; height:2.843ex;" alt=3D"{\displaystyle x^=
{16}+x^{14}+x^{13}+x^{11}+1.}"></span></dd></dl>
<p>The "one" in the polynomial does not correspond to a tap =E2=80=93 it co=
rresponds to the input to the first bit (i.e. <i>x</i><sup>0</sup>, which i=
s equivalent to 1). The powers of the terms represent the tapped bits, coun=
ting from the left. The first and last bits are always connected as an inpu=
t and output tap respectively.
</p><p>The LFSR is maximal-length if and only if the corresponding feedback=
 polynomial is <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primitive_polynomia=
l_(field_theory)" title=3D"Primitive polynomial (field theory)">primitive</=
a> over the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finite_field" title=3D=
"Finite field">Galois field</a> GF(2).<sup id=3D"cite_ref-3" class=3D"refer=
ence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear-feedback_shift_regist=
er#cite_note-3"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>3<span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-4" class=3D"reference"><a hre=
f=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear-feedback_shift_register#cite_note=
-4"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>4<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</s=
pan></a></sup> This means that the following conditions are necessary (but =
not sufficient):
</p>
<ul><li>The number of taps is <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Even=
_and_odd_numbers" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Even and odd numbers">even=
</a>.</li>
<li>The set of taps is <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coprime_int=
egers#Coprimality_in_sets" title=3D"Coprime integers">setwise co-prime</a>;=
 i.e., there must be no divisor other than 1 common to all taps.</li></ul>
<p>Tables of primitive polynomials from which maximum-length LFSRs can be c=
onstructed are given below and in the references.
</p><p>There can be more than one maximum-length tap sequence for a given L=
FSR length. Also, once one maximum-length tap sequence has been found, anot=
her automatically follows. If the tap sequence in an <i>n</i>-bit LFSR is <=
span class=3D"nowrap">[<i>n</i>, <i>A</i>, <i>B</i>, <i>C</i>, 0]</span>, w=
here the 0 corresponds to the <i>x</i><sup>0</sup>&nbsp;=3D&nbsp;1 term, th=
en the corresponding "mirror" sequence is <span class=3D"nowrap">[<i>n</i>,=
 <i>n</i> =E2=88=92 <i>C</i>, <i>n</i> =E2=88=92 <i>B</i>, <i>n</i> =E2=88=
=92 <i>A</i>, 0]</span>. So the tap sequence <span class=3D"nowrap">[32, 22=
, 2, 1, 0]</span> has as its counterpart <span class=3D"nowrap">[32, 31, 30=
, 10, 0]</span>. Both give a maximum-length sequence.
</p><p>An example in <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_(programmin=
g_language)" title=3D"C (programming language)">C</a> is below:
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-c mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">=
<pre><span></span><span class=3D"cp">#include</span><span class=3D"w"> </sp=
an><span class=3D"cpf">&lt;stdint.h&gt;</span>
<span class=3D"kt">unsigned</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"=
nf">lfsr_fib</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">void</span>=
<span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"p">{</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"kt">uint16_t</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">start_state</span><span class=3D"w"> </spa=
n><span class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mh">=
0xACE1u</span><span class=3D"p">;</span><span class=3D"w">  </span><span cl=
ass=3D"cm">/* Any nonzero start state will work. */</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"kt">uint16_t</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span=
 class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">start_st=
ate</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"kt">uint16_t</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">bit</span><span class=3D"p">;</span><span =
class=3D"w">                    </span><span class=3D"cm">/* Must be 16-bit=
 to allow bit&lt;&lt;15 later in the code */</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"kt">unsigned</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">period</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><sp=
an class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mi">0</sp=
an><span class=3D"p">;</span>

<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">do</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"p">{</span><span class=3D"w">  =
 </span><span class=3D"cm">/* taps: 16 14 13 11; feedback polynomial: x^16 =
+ x^14 + x^13 + x^11 + 1 */</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"n">bit</span><span class=3D=
"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span cla=
ss=3D"p">((</span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><s=
pan class=3D"o">&gt;&gt;</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mi"=
>0</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D=
"o">^</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=
=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">&gt;&gt;</spa=
n><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mi">2</span><span class=3D"p">)<=
/span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">^</span><span class=3D"w"=
> </span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">&gt;&gt;</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><=
span class=3D"mi">3</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><span class=3D"w"> </sp=
an><span class=3D"o">^</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"p">(<=
/span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D=
"o">&gt;&gt;</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mi">5</span><sp=
an class=3D"p">))</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">&amp;</=
span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mi">1u</span><span class=3D"p=
">;</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span =
class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </span>=
<span class=3D"o">&gt;&gt;</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"m=
i">1</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=
=3D"o">|</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span cl=
ass=3D"n">bit</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">&lt;&lt;</s=
pan><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mi">15</span><span class=3D"p"=
>);</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"o">++</span><span class=3D"=
n">period</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"p">}</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">while</span><span class=3D"w=
"> </span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span clas=
s=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">!=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><spa=
n class=3D"n">start_state</span><span class=3D"p">);</span>

<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">return</span><span class=3D"=
w"> </span><span class=3D"n">period</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</pre></div>
<p>If a fast <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parity_function" titl=
e=3D"Parity function">parity</a> or <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Popcount" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Popcount">popcount</a> operation=
 is available, the feedback bit can be computed more efficiently as the <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dot_product" title=3D"Dot product">do=
t product</a> of the register with the characteristic polynomial:
</p>
<ul><li><code class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-c mw-content-ltr" dir=
=3D"ltr"><span class=3D"n">bit</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=
=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">parity</span><=
span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </=
span><span class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D=
"mh">0x002Du</span><span class=3D"p">);</span></code>, or equivalently</li>
<li><code class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-c mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"=
ltr"><span class=3D"n">bit</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o=
">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">popcnt</span><span =
class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </span>=
<span class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mh">=
0x002Du</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span cla=
ss=3D"cm">/* &amp; 1u */</span><span class=3D"p">;</span></code>. (The <cod=
e>&amp; 1u</code> turns the popcnt into a true parity function, but the bit=
shift later <code>bit &lt;&lt; 15</code> makes higher bits irrelevant.)</li=
></ul>
<p>If a rotation operation is available, the new state can be computed as
</p>
<ul><li><code class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-c mw-content-ltr" dir=
=3D"ltr"><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=
=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">rotateright</s=
pan><span class=3D"p">((</span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"=
w"> </span><span class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span cl=
ass=3D"o">~</span><span class=3D"mi">1u</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><sp=
an class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">|</span><span class=3D"w"> </span>=
<span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">bit</span><span class=3D"w"> </=
span><span class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D=
"mi">1u</span><span class=3D"p">),</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span cl=
ass=3D"mi">1</span><span class=3D"p">);</span></code>, or equivalently</li>
<li><code class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-c mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"=
ltr"><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"=
o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">rotateright</span>=
<span class=3D"p">(((</span><span class=3D"n">bit</span><span class=3D"w"> =
</span><span class=3D"o">^</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n=
">lfsr</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span clas=
s=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mi">1u</span><=
span class=3D"p">)</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">^</spa=
n><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"p">=
,</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mi">1</span><span class=3D=
"p">);</span></code></li></ul>
<p>This LFSR configuration is also known as <b>standard</b>, <b>many-to-one=
</b> or <b>external XOR gates</b>. The alternative Galois configuration is =
described in the next section.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Example_in_Python">Example =
in Python</h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-=
bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DLi=
near-feedback_shift_register&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D2" title=3D"Ed=
it section: Example in Python"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-edits=
ection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>A sample python implementation of a similar (16 bit taps at [16,15,13,4]=
) Fibonacci LFSR would be
</p>
<div style=3D"clear:both;" class=3D""></div>
<div class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-python mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"=
ltr"><pre><span></span><span class=3D"n">start_state</span> <span class=3D"=
o">=3D</span> <span class=3D"mi">1</span> <span class=3D"o">&lt;&lt;</span>=
 <span class=3D"mi">15</span> <span class=3D"o">|</span> <span class=3D"mi"=
>1</span>
<span class=3D"n">lfsr</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=3D"n=
">start_state</span>
<span class=3D"n">period</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=3D=
"mi">0</span>

<span class=3D"k">while</span> <span class=3D"kc">True</span><span class=3D=
"p">:</span>
    <span class=3D"c1"># taps: 16 15 13 4; feedback polynomial: x^16 + x^15=
 + x^13 + x^4 + 1</span>
    <span class=3D"n">bit</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=
=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span> <span class=3D"o">^</span> <sp=
an class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span> <span class=3D"o">&gt;=
&gt;</span> <span class=3D"mi">1</span><span class=3D"p">)</span> <span cla=
ss=3D"o">^</span> <span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span> <=
span class=3D"o">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class=3D"mi">3</span><span class=3D"=
p">)</span> <span class=3D"o">^</span> <span class=3D"p">(</span><span clas=
s=3D"n">lfsr</span> <span class=3D"o">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class=3D"mi">12=
</span><span class=3D"p">))</span> <span class=3D"o">&amp;</span> <span cla=
ss=3D"mi">1</span>
    <span class=3D"n">lfsr</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=
=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span> <span class=3D"o">&gt;&gt;</sp=
an> <span class=3D"mi">1</span><span class=3D"p">)</span> <span class=3D"o"=
>|</span> <span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">bit</span> <span clas=
s=3D"o">&lt;&lt;</span> <span class=3D"mi">15</span><span class=3D"p">)</sp=
an>
    <span class=3D"n">period</span> <span class=3D"o">+=3D</span> <span cla=
ss=3D"mi">1</span>
    <span class=3D"k">if</span> <span class=3D"n">lfsr</span> <span class=
=3D"o">=3D=3D</span> <span class=3D"n">start_state</span><span class=3D"p">=
:</span>
        <span class=3D"nb">print</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span clas=
s=3D"n">period</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
        <span class=3D"k">break</span>
</pre></div>
<p>Where a register of 16 bits is used and the xor tap at the fourth, 13th,=
 15th and sixteenth bit establishes a maximum sequence length.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Galois_LFSRs">Galois LFSRs<=
/h2><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[=
</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DLinear-feedb=
ack_shift_register&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D3" title=3D"Edit section=
: Galois LFSRs"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket"=
>]</span></span></div>
<figure class=3D"mw-halign-right" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:LFSR-G16.svg" class=3D"mw-file-description"><=
img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/LFSR-G=
16.svg/500px-LFSR-G16.svg.png" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"393" height=3D"1=
20" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-file-width=3D"393" data-file-height=3D"1=
20"></a><figcaption>A 16-bit Galois LFSR. The register numbers above corres=
pond to the same primitive polynomial as the Fibonacci example but are coun=
ted in reverse to the shifting direction. This register also cycles through=
 the maximal number of 65535 states excluding the all-zeroes state. The sta=
te ACE1 hex shown will be followed by E270 hex.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Named after the French mathematician <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/%C3%89variste_Galois" title=3D"=C3=89variste Galois">=C3=89variste Ga=
lois</a>, an LFSR in Galois configuration, which is also known as <b>modula=
r</b>, <b>internal XORs</b>, or <b>one-to-many LFSR</b>, is an alternate st=
ructure that can generate the same output stream as a conventional LFSR (bu=
t offset in time).<sup id=3D"cite_ref-5" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear-feedback_shift_register#cite_note-5"><spa=
n class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>5<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a>=
</sup> In the Galois configuration, when the system is clocked, bits that a=
re not taps are shifted one position to the right unchanged. The taps, on t=
he other hand, are XORed with the output bit before they are stored in the =
next position. The new output bit is the next input bit. The effect of this=
 is that when the output bit is zero, all the bits in the register shift to=
 the right unchanged, and the input bit becomes zero. When the output bit i=
s one, the bits in the tap positions all flip (if they are 0, they become 1=
, and if they are 1, they become 0), and then the entire register is shifte=
d to the right and the input bit becomes 1.
</p><p>To generate the same output stream, the order of the taps is the <i>=
counterpart</i> (see above) of the order for the conventional LFSR, otherwi=
se the stream will be in reverse. Note that the internal state of the LFSR =
is not necessarily the same. The Galois register shown has the same output =
stream as the Fibonacci register in the first section. A time offset exists=
 between the streams, so a different startpoint will be needed to get the s=
ame output each cycle.
</p>
<ul><li>Galois LFSRs do not concatenate every tap to produce the new input =
(the XORing is done within the LFSR, and no XOR gates are run in serial, th=
erefore the propagation times are reduced to that of one XOR rather than a =
whole chain), thus it is possible for each tap to be computed in parallel, =
increasing the speed of execution.</li>
<li>In a software implementation of an LFSR, the Galois form is more effici=
ent, as the XOR operations can be implemented a word at a time: only the ou=
tput bit must be examined individually.</li></ul>
<p>Below is a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_(programming_langu=
age)" title=3D"C (programming language)">C</a> code example for the 16-bit =
maximal-period Galois LFSR example in the figure:
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-c mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">=
<pre><span></span><span class=3D"cp">#include</span><span class=3D"w"> </sp=
an><span class=3D"cpf">&lt;stdint.h&gt;</span>
<span class=3D"kt">unsigned</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"=
nf">lfsr_galois</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">void</sp=
an><span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"p">{</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"kt">uint16_t</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">start_state</span><span class=3D"w"> </spa=
n><span class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mh">=
0xACE1u</span><span class=3D"p">;</span><span class=3D"w">  </span><span cl=
ass=3D"cm">/* Any nonzero start state will work. */</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"kt">uint16_t</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span=
 class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">start_st=
ate</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"kt">unsigned</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">period</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><sp=
an class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mi">0</sp=
an><span class=3D"p">;</span>

<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">do</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"p">{</span>
<span class=3D"cp">#ifndef LEFT</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"kt">unsigned</span><span cl=
ass=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">lsb</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><sp=
an class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</=
span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=3D=
"w"> </span><span class=3D"mi">1u</span><span class=3D"p">;</span><span cla=
ss=3D"w">  </span><span class=3D"cm">/* Get LSB (i.e., the output bit). */<=
/span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">&gt;&gt;=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </spa=
n><span class=3D"mi">1</span><span class=3D"p">;</span><span class=3D"w">  =
              </span><span class=3D"cm">/* Shift register */</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"k">if</span><span class=3D"=
w"> </span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">lsb</span><span clas=
s=3D"p">)</span><span class=3D"w">                   </span><span class=3D"=
cm">/* If the output bit is 1, */</span>
<span class=3D"w">            </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span cla=
ss=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">^=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><sp=
an class=3D"mh">0xB400u</span><span class=3D"p">;</span><span class=3D"w"> =
      </span><span class=3D"cm">/*  apply toggle mask. */</span>
<span class=3D"cp">#else</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"kt">unsigned</span><span cl=
ass=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">msb</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><sp=
an class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"p">(</spa=
n><span class=3D"kt">int16_t</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><span class=3D=
"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span cl=
ass=3D"o">&lt;</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mi">0</span><=
span class=3D"p">;</span><span class=3D"w">   </span><span class=3D"cm">/* =
Get MSB (i.e., the output bit). */</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">&lt;&lt;=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </spa=
n><span class=3D"mi">1</span><span class=3D"p">;</span><span class=3D"w">  =
                        </span><span class=3D"cm">/* Shift register */</spa=
n>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"k">if</span><span class=3D"=
w"> </span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">msb</span><span clas=
s=3D"p">)</span><span class=3D"w">                             </span><span=
 class=3D"cm">/* If the output bit is 1, */</span>
<span class=3D"w">            </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span cla=
ss=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">^=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><sp=
an class=3D"mh">0x002Du</span><span class=3D"p">;</span><span class=3D"w"> =
                </span><span class=3D"cm">/*  apply toggle mask. */</span>
<span class=3D"cp">#endif</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"o">++</span><span class=3D"=
n">period</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"p">}</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">while</span><span class=3D"w=
"> </span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span clas=
s=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">!=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><spa=
n class=3D"n">start_state</span><span class=3D"p">);</span>

<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">return</span><span class=3D"=
w"> </span><span class=3D"n">period</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</pre></div>
<p>The branch <code class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-c mw-content-lt=
r" dir=3D"ltr"><span class=3D"k">if</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span c=
lass=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">lsb</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><s=
pan class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </s=
pan><span class=3D"o">^=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"m=
h">0xB400u</span><span class=3D"p">;</span></code>can also be written as <c=
ode class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-c mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr"><=
span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">^=
=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=
=3D"o">-</span><span class=3D"n">lsb</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><span =
class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=3D"w"> </span=
><span class=3D"mh">0xB400u</span><span class=3D"p">;</span></code> which m=
ay produce more efficient code on some compilers. In addition, the left-shi=
fting variant may produce even better code, as the <a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Most_significant_bit" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Most =
significant bit">msb</a> is the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ca=
rry_flag" title=3D"Carry flag">carry</a> from the addition of <code>lfsr</c=
ode> to itself.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Galois_LFSR_parallel_comput=
ation">Galois LFSR parallel computation</h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection">=
<span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/w/index.php?title=3DLinear-feedback_shift_register&amp;action=3Dedi=
t&amp;section=3D4" title=3D"Edit section: Galois LFSR parallel computation"=
><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span=
></div>
<p>State and resulting bits can also be combined and computed in parallel. =
The following function calculates the next 64 bits using the 63-bit polynom=
ial <span class=3D"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline"><span class=3D=
"mwe-math-mathml-inline mwe-math-mathml-a11y" style=3D"display: none;"><mat=
h xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext=3D"{\displaystyle x^=
{63}+x^{62}+1}">
  <semantics>
    <mrow class=3D"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
      <mstyle displaystyle=3D"true" scriptlevel=3D"0">
        <msup>
          <mi>x</mi>
          <mrow class=3D"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mn>63</mn>
          </mrow>
        </msup>
        <mo>+</mo>
        <msup>
          <mi>x</mi>
          <mrow class=3D"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mn>62</mn>
          </mrow>
        </msup>
        <mo>+</mo>
        <mn>1</mn>
      </mstyle>
    </mrow>
    <annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">{\displaystyle x^{63}+x^{62}=
+1}</annotation>
  </semantics>
</math></span><img src=3D"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/rend=
er/svg/0f3cc1b88515ddc7e297c52fda894314c6f6b3f1" class=3D"mwe-math-fallback=
-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert" aria-hidden=3D"true" style=3D"vertical=
-align: -0.505ex; width:13.255ex; height:2.843ex;" alt=3D"{\displaystyle x^=
{63}+x^{62}+1}"></span>:
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-c mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">=
<pre><span></span><span class=3D"cp">#include</span><span class=3D"w"> </sp=
an><span class=3D"cpf">&lt;stdint.h&gt;</span>

<span class=3D"kt">uint64_t</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"=
nf">prsg63</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">uint64_t</spa=
n><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"p">=
)</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"p">{</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"=
> </span><span class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=
=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">&lt;&lt;</spa=
n><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mi">32</span><span class=3D"w"> =
</span><span class=3D"o">|</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"p=
">(</span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"o">&lt;&lt;</span><sp=
an class=3D"mi">1</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">^</span=
><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"o">&=
lt;&lt;</span><span class=3D"mi">2</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><span cl=
ass=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">&gt;&gt;</span><span class=3D"w"> </spa=
n><span class=3D"mi">32</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"=
> </span><span class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=
=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">&lt;&lt;</spa=
n><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mi">32</span><span class=3D"w"> =
</span><span class=3D"o">|</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"p=
">(</span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"o">&lt;&lt;</span><sp=
an class=3D"mi">1</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">^</span=
><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"o">&=
lt;&lt;</span><span class=3D"mi">2</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><span cl=
ass=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">&gt;&gt;</span><span class=3D"w"> </spa=
n><span class=3D"mi">32</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">return</span><span class=3D"=
w"> </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</pre></div>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Non-binary_Galois_LFSR">Non=
-binary Galois LFSR</h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-ed=
itsection-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?=
title=3DLinear-feedback_shift_register&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D5" t=
itle=3D"Edit section: Non-binary Galois LFSR"><span>edit</span></a><span cl=
ass=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>Binary Galois LFSRs like the ones shown above can be generalized to any =
<i>q</i>-ary alphabet {0, 1, ..., <i>q</i>&nbsp;=E2=88=92&nbsp;1} (e.g., fo=
r binary, <i>q</i> =3D 2, and the alphabet is simply {0, 1}). In this case,=
 the exclusive-or component is generalized to addition <a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modular_arithmetic" title=3D"Modular arithmetic">modul=
o</a>-<i>q</i> (note that XOR is addition modulo 2), and the feedback bit (=
output bit) is multiplied (modulo-<i>q</i>) by a <i>q</i>-ary value, which =
is constant for each specific tap point. Note that this is also a generaliz=
ation of the binary case, where the feedback is multiplied by either 0 (no =
feedback, i.e., no tap) or 1 (feedback is present). Given an appropriate ta=
p configuration, such LFSRs can be used to generate <a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Finite_field" title=3D"Finite field">Galois fields</a> fo=
r arbitrary prime values of <i>q</i>.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Xorshift_LFSRs">Xorshift LF=
SRs</h2><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracke=
t">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DLinear-f=
eedback_shift_register&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D6" title=3D"Edit sec=
tion: Xorshift LFSRs"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-br=
acket">]</span></span></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main article: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xorshift" title=3D"X=
orshift">Xorshift</a></div>
<p>As shown by <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Marsaglia" t=
itle=3D"George Marsaglia">George Marsaglia</a><sup id=3D"cite_ref-marsaglia=
_6-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear-f=
eedback_shift_register#cite_note-marsaglia-6"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
[</span>6<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> and further analys=
ed by <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_P._Brent" title=3D"R=
ichard P. Brent">Richard P. Brent</a>,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-brent_7-0" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear-feedback_shi=
ft_register#cite_note-brent-7"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>7<span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> linear feedback shift registers c=
an be implemented using XOR and Shift operations. This approach lends itsel=
f to fast execution in software because these operations typically map effi=
ciently into modern processor instructions.
</p><p>Below is a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_(programming_l=
anguage)" title=3D"C (programming language)">C</a> code example for a 16-bi=
t maximal-period Xorshift LFSR using the 7,9,13 triplet from John Metcalf:<=
sup id=3D"cite_ref-8" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Linear-feedback_shift_register#cite_note-8"><span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket">[</span>8<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-highlight mw-highlight-lang-c mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">=
<pre><span></span><span class=3D"cp">#include</span><span class=3D"w"> </sp=
an><span class=3D"cpf">&lt;stdint.h&gt;</span>
<span class=3D"kt">unsigned</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"=
nf">lfsr_xorshift</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">void</=
span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"p">{</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"kt">uint16_t</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">start_state</span><span class=3D"w"> </spa=
n><span class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mh">=
0xACE1u</span><span class=3D"p">;</span><span class=3D"w">  </span><span cl=
ass=3D"cm">/* Any nonzero start state will work. */</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"kt">uint16_t</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span=
 class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">start_st=
ate</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"kt">unsigned</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">period</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><sp=
an class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mi">0</sp=
an><span class=3D"p">;</span>

<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">do</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"p">{</span><span class=3D"w">  =
 </span><span class=3D"c1">// 7,9,13 triplet from <a class=3D"code-link" hr=
ef=3D"http://www.retroprogramming.com/2017/07/xorshift-pseudorandom-numbers=
-in-z80.html">http://www.retroprogramming.com/2017/07/xorshift-pseudorandom=
-numbers-in-z80.html</a></span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">^=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span=
 class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">&gt;&gt=
;</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mi">7</span><span class=3D=
"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">^=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span=
 class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">&lt;&lt=
;</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mi">9</span><span class=3D=
"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=
=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">^=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span=
 class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">&gt;&gt=
;</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"mi">13</span><span class=
=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"w">        </span><span class=3D"o">++</span><span class=3D"=
n">period</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"p">}</span>
<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">while</span><span class=3D"w=
"> </span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">lfsr</span><span clas=
s=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">!=3D</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><spa=
n class=3D"n">start_state</span><span class=3D"p">);</span>

<span class=3D"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">return</span><span class=3D"=
w"> </span><span class=3D"n">period</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</pre></div>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Matrix_forms">Matrix forms<=
/h2><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[=
</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DLinear-feedb=
ack_shift_register&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D7" title=3D"Edit section=
: Matrix forms"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket"=
>]</span></span></div>
<p>Binary LFSRs of both Fibonacci and Galois configurations can be expresse=
d as linear functions using matrices in <span class=3D"mwe-math-element mwe=
-math-element-inline"><span class=3D"mwe-math-mathml-inline mwe-math-mathml=
-a11y" style=3D"display: none;"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/=
MathML" alttext=3D"{\displaystyle \mathbb {F} _{2}}">
  <semantics>
    <mrow class=3D"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
      <mstyle displaystyle=3D"true" scriptlevel=3D"0">
        <msub>
          <mrow class=3D"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mi mathvariant=3D"double-struck">F</mi>
          </mrow>
          <mrow class=3D"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mn>2</mn>
          </mrow>
        </msub>
      </mstyle>
    </mrow>
    <annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">{\displaystyle \mathbb {F} _=
{2}}</annotation>
  </semantics>
</math></span><img src=3D"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/rend=
er/svg/fde97f1e971e76227cd0aac645b7b0901d7b668d" class=3D"mwe-math-fallback=
-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert" aria-hidden=3D"true" style=3D"vertical=
-align: -0.671ex; width:2.475ex; height:2.509ex;" alt=3D"{\displaystyle \ma=
thbb {F} _{2}}"></span> (see <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GF(2)=
" title=3D"GF(2)">GF(2)</a>).<sup id=3D"cite_ref-9" class=3D"reference"><a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear-feedback_shift_register#cite_n=
ote-9"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>9<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]=
</span></a></sup> Using the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compan=
ion_matrix" title=3D"Companion matrix">companion matrix</a> of the characte=
ristic polynomial of the LFSR and denoting the seed as a column vector <spa=
n class=3D"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline"><span class=3D"mwe-mat=
h-mathml-inline mwe-math-mathml-a11y" style=3D"display: none;"><math xmlns=
=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext=3D"{\displaystyle (a_{0},a_=
{1},\dots ,a_{n-1})^{\mathrm {T} }}">
  <semantics>
    <mrow class=3D"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
      <mstyle displaystyle=3D"true" scriptlevel=3D"0">
        <mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo>
        <msub>
          <mi>a</mi>
          <mrow class=3D"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mn>0</mn>
          </mrow>
        </msub>
        <mo>,</mo>
        <msub>
          <mi>a</mi>
          <mrow class=3D"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mn>1</mn>
          </mrow>
        </msub>
        <mo>,</mo>
        <mo>=E2=80=A6<!-- =E2=80=A6 --></mo>
        <mo>,</mo>
        <msub>
          <mi>a</mi>
          <mrow class=3D"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mi>n</mi>
            <mo>=E2=88=92<!-- =E2=88=92 --></mo>
            <mn>1</mn>
          </mrow>
        </msub>
        <msup>
          <mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo>
          <mrow class=3D"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
            <mrow class=3D"MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
              <mi mathvariant=3D"normal">T</mi>
            </mrow>
          </mrow>
        </msup>
      </mstyle>
    </mrow>
    <annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">{\displaystyle (a_{0},a_{1},=
\dots ,a_{n-1})^{\mathrm {T} }}</annotation>
  </semantics>
</math></span><img src=3D"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/rend=
er/svg/7afe5317c6b801675732464d09c3d58081534b44" class=3D"mwe-math-fallback=
