\begin{pmatrix}c_{n-1}&amp;1&amp;0&amp;\cdots &amp;0\\c_{n-2}&amp;0&amp;1&a=
mp;\ddots &amp;\vdots \\\vdots &amp;\vdots &amp;\ddots &amp;\ddots &amp;0\\=
c_{1}&amp;0&amp;\cdots &amp;0&amp;1\\c_{0}&amp;0&amp;\cdots &amp;\cdots &am=
p;0\end{pmatrix}}^{k}{\begin{pmatrix}a'_{0}\\a'_{1}\\a'_{2}\\\vdots \\a'_{n=
-1}\end{pmatrix}}}"></span></dd></dl>
<p>gives the term <span class=3D"texhtml"><i>a</i><sub><i>k</i></sub></span=
> of the original sequence.
</p><p>These forms generalize naturally to arbitrary fields.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Example_polynomials_for_max=
imal_LFSRs">Example polynomials for maximal LFSRs</h2><span class=3D"mw-edi=
tsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DLinear-feedback_shift_register&amp;ac=
tion=3Dedit&amp;section=3D8" title=3D"Edit section: Example polynomials for=
 maximal LFSRs"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket"=
>]</span></span></div>
<p>The following table lists examples of maximal-length feedback polynomial=
s (<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primitive_polynomial_(field_the=
ory)" title=3D"Primitive polynomial (field theory)">primitive polynomials</=
a>) for shift-register lengths up to 24. The formalism for maximum-length L=
FSRs was developed by <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solomon_W._G=
olomb" title=3D"Solomon W. Golomb">Solomon W. Golomb</a> in his 1967 book.<=
sup id=3D"cite_ref-10" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Linear-feedback_shift_register#cite_note-10"><span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">[</span>10<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The numbe=
r of different <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primitive_polynomia=
l_(field_theory)" title=3D"Primitive polynomial (field theory)">primitive p=
olynomials</a> grows exponentially with shift-register length and can be ca=
lculated exactly using <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler%27s_t=
otient_function" title=3D"Euler's totient function">Euler's totient functio=
n</a><sup id=3D"cite_ref-11" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Linear-feedback_shift_register#cite_note-11"><span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">[</span>11<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> (se=
quence <span class=3D"nowrap external"><a href=3D"https://oeis.org/A011260"=
 class=3D"extiw" title=3D"oeis:A011260">A011260</a></span> in the <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/On-Line_Encyclopedia_of_Integer_Sequences=
" title=3D"On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences">OEIS</a>).
</p>
<table class=3D"wikitable" style=3D"text-align:right">

<tbody><tr>
