<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Output-stream_properties">O=
utput-stream properties</h2><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"m=
w-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.=
php?title=3DLinear-feedback_shift_register&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D=
9" title=3D"Edit section: Output-stream properties"><span>edit</span></a><s=
pan class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<ul><li>Ones and zeroes occur in "runs". The output stream 1110010, for exa=
mple, consists of four runs of lengths 3, 2, 1, 1, in order. In one period =
of a maximal LFSR, 2<sup><i>n</i>=E2=88=921</sup> runs occur (in the exampl=
e above, the 3-bit LFSR has 4 runs). Exactly half of these runs are one bit=
 long, a quarter are two bits long, up to a single run of zeroes <i>n</i>&n=
bsp;=E2=88=92&nbsp;1 bits long, and a single run of ones <i>n</i> bits long=
. This distribution almost equals the statistical <a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Expected_value" title=3D"Expected value">expectation value<=
/a> for a truly random sequence. However, the probability of finding exactl=
y this distribution in a sample of a truly random sequence is rather low<su=
p class=3D"noprint Inline-Template" style=3D"margin-left:0.1em; white-space=
:nowrap;">[<i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Vagueness"=
 title=3D"Wikipedia:Vagueness"><span title=3D"This information is too vague=
. (April 2013)">vague</span></a></i>]</sup>.</li>
<li>LFSR output streams are <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Determ=
inistic" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Deterministic">deterministic</a>. I=
f the present state and the positions of the XOR gates in the LFSR are know=
n, the next state can be predicted.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-xilinx.com_12-0" cla=
ss=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear-feedback_s=
hift_register#cite_note-xilinx.com-12"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span=
>12<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> This is not possible wit=
h truly random events. With maximal-length LFSRs, it is much easier to comp=
ute the next state, as there are only an easily limited number of them for =
each length.</li>
<li>The output stream is reversible; an LFSR with mirrored taps will cycle =
through the output sequence in reverse order.</li>
<li>The value consisting of all zeros cannot appear. Thus an LFSR of length=
 <i>n</i> cannot be used to generate all 2<sup><i>n</i></sup> values.</li><=
/ul>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Applications">Applications<=
/h2><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[=
</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DLinear-feedb=
ack_shift_register&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D10" title=3D"Edit sectio=
n: Applications"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket=
">]</span></span></div>
<p>LFSRs can be implemented in hardware, and this makes them useful in appl=
ications that require very fast generation of a pseudo-random sequence, suc=
h as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Direct-sequence_spread_spectr=
um" title=3D"Direct-sequence spread spectrum">direct-sequence spread spectr=
um</a> radio. LFSRs have also been used for generating an approximation of =
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_noise" title=3D"White noise"=
>white noise</a> in various <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Progra=
mmable_sound_generator" title=3D"Programmable sound generator">programmable=
 sound generators</a>.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Uses_as_counters">Uses as c=
ounters</h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-br=
acket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DLine=
ar-feedback_shift_register&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D11" title=3D"Edi=
t section: Uses as counters"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsec=
tion-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>The repeating sequence of states of an LFSR allows it to be used as a <a=
 href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clock_divider" class=3D"mw-redirect"=
 title=3D"Clock divider">clock divider</a> or as a counter when a non-binar=
y sequence is acceptable, as is often the case where computer index or fram=
ing locations need to be machine-readable.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-xilinx.com_12=
-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear-fee=
dback_shift_register#cite_note-xilinx.com-12"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
[</span>12<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> LFSR <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Counter_(digital)" title=3D"Counter (digital)"=
>counters</a> have simpler feedback logic than natural binary counters or <=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gray-code_counter" class=3D"mw-redi=
rect" title=3D"Gray-code counter">Gray-code counters</a>, and therefore can=
 operate at higher clock rates. However, it is necessary to ensure that the=
 LFSR never enters a lockup state (all zeros for a XOR based LFSR, and all =
ones for a XNOR based LFSR), for example by presetting it at start-up to an=
y other state in the sequence. It is possible to count up and down with a L=
FSR. LFSR have also been used as a <a rel=3D"nofollow" class=3D"external te=
xt" href=3D"https://github.com/howerj/lfsr-vhdl">Program Counter for CPUs</=
a>, this requires that the program itself is "scrambled" and it done to sav=
e on gates when they are a premium (using fewer gates than an adder) and fo=
r speed (as a LFSR does not require a long carry chain).
</p><p>The table of primitive polynomials shows how LFSRs can be arranged i=
n Fibonacci or Galois form to give maximal periods. One can obtain any othe=
r period by adding to an LFSR that has a longer period some logic that shor=
tens the sequence by skipping some states.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Uses_in_cryptography">Uses =
in cryptography</h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editse=
ction-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?titl=
e=3DLinear-feedback_shift_register&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D12" titl=
e=3D"Edit section: Uses in cryptography"><span>edit</span></a><span class=
=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>LFSRs have long been used as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ps=
eudo-random_number_generator" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Pseudo-random =
number generator">pseudo-random number generators</a> for use in <a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stream_cipher" title=3D"Stream cipher">strea=
m ciphers</a>, due to the ease of construction from simple <a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electromechanical" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"=
Electromechanical">electromechanical</a> or <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Electronic_circuits" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Electronic ci=
rcuits">electronic circuits</a>, long <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Periodic_function" title=3D"Periodic function">periods</a>, and very un=
iformly <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Probability_distribution" =
title=3D"Probability distribution">distributed</a> output streams. However,=
 an LFSR is a linear system, leading to fairly easy <a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Cryptanalysis" title=3D"Cryptanalysis">cryptanalysis</a>.=
 For example, given a stretch of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K=
nown-plaintext_attack" title=3D"Known-plaintext attack">known plaintext and=
 corresponding ciphertext</a>, an attacker can intercept and recover a stre=
tch of LFSR output stream used in the system described, and from that stret=
ch of the output stream can construct an LFSR of minimal size that simulate=
s the intended receiver by using the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Berlekamp-Massey_algorithm" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Berlekamp-Mas=
sey algorithm">Berlekamp-Massey algorithm</a>. This LFSR can then be fed th=
e intercepted stretch of output stream to recover the remaining plaintext.
</p><p>Three general methods are employed to reduce this problem in LFSR-ba=
sed stream ciphers:
</p>
<ul><li><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-linear" class=3D"mw-re=
direct" title=3D"Non-linear">Non-linear</a> combination of several <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bit" title=3D"Bit">bits</a> from the LFSR=
 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_(computer_science)" title=
=3D"State (computer science)">state</a>;</li>
<li>Non-linear combination of the output bits of two or more LFSRs (see als=
o: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shrinking_generator" title=3D"S=
hrinking generator">shrinking generator</a>); or using <a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evolutionary_algorithm" title=3D"Evolutionary algorith=
m">Evolutionary algorithm</a> to introduce non-linearity.<sup id=3D"cite_re=
f-13" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear-f=
eedback_shift_register#cite_note-13"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>1=
3<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup></li>
<li>Irregular clocking of the LFSR, as in the <a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Alternating_step_generator" title=3D"Alternating step generator=
">alternating step generator</a>.</li></ul>
<p>Important LFSR-based stream ciphers include <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/A5/1" title=3D"A5/1">A5/1</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/A5/2" title=3D"A5/2">A5/2</a>, used in <a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/GSM" title=3D"GSM">GSM</a> cell phones, <a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/E0_(cipher)" title=3D"E0 (cipher)">E0</a>, used in =
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bluetooth" title=3D"Bluetooth">Blu=
etooth</a>, and the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shrinking_gene=
rator" title=3D"Shrinking generator">shrinking generator</a>. The A5/2 ciph=
er has been broken and both A5/1 and E0 have serious weaknesses.<sup id=3D"=
cite_ref-14" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L=
inear-feedback_shift_register#cite_note-14"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[<=
/span>14<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-=
15" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear-fee=
dback_shift_register#cite_note-15"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>15<=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>The linear feedback shift register has a strong relationship to <a h=
ref=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_congruential_generator" title=
=3D"Linear congruential generator">linear congruential generators</a>.<sup =
id=3D"cite_ref-16" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Linear-feedback_shift_register#cite_note-16"><span class=3D"cite-brack=
et">[</span>16<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Uses_in_circuit_testing">Us=
es in circuit testing</h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-=
editsection-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.ph=
p?title=3DLinear-feedback_shift_register&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D13=
" title=3D"Edit section: Uses in circuit testing"><span>edit</span></a><spa=
n class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1305433154"><table class=3D"box-More_citations_needed_section plainlinks me=
tadata ambox ambox-content ambox-Refimprove" role=3D"presentation"><tbody><=
tr><td class=3D"mbox-image"><div class=3D"mbox-image-div"><span typeof=3D"m=
w:File"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Question_book-new.svg=
" class=3D"mw-file-description"><img alt=3D"icon" src=3D"https://upload.wik=
imedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/99/Question_book-new.svg/60px-Question_book=
-new.svg.png" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"50" height=3D"39" class=3D"mw-fil=
e-element" data-file-width=3D"512" data-file-height=3D"399"></a></span></di=
v></td><td class=3D"mbox-text"><div class=3D"mbox-text-span">This section <=
b>needs additional citations for <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W=
ikipedia:Verifiability" title=3D"Wikipedia:Verifiability">verification</a><=
/b>.<span class=3D"hide-when-compact"> Please help <a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Special:EditPage/Linear-feedback_shift_register" title=3D"=
Special:EditPage/Linear-feedback shift register">improve this article</a> b=
y <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Referencing_for_beginners" =
title=3D"Help:Referencing for beginners">adding citations to reliable sourc=
es</a> in this section. Unsourced material may be challenged and removed.</=
span>  <span class=3D"date-container"><i>(<span class=3D"date">November 202=
2</span>)</i></span><span class=3D"hide-when-compact"><i> (<small><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Maintenance_template_removal" title=
=3D"Help:Maintenance template removal">Learn how and when to remove this me=
ssage</a></small>)</i></span></div></td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>LFSRs are used in circuit testing for test-pattern generation (for exhau=
stive testing, pseudo-random testing or pseudo-exhaustive testing) and for =
signature analysis.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Test-pattern_generation">Te=
st-pattern generation</h4><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-=
editsection-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.ph=
p?title=3DLinear-feedback_shift_register&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D14=
" title=3D"Edit section: Test-pattern generation"><span>edit</span></a><spa=
n class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>Complete LFSR are commonly used as pattern generators for exhaustive tes=
ting, since they cover all possible inputs for an <i>n</i>-input circuit. M=
aximal-length LFSRs and weighted LFSRs are widely used as pseudo-random tes=
t-pattern generators for pseudo-random test applications.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Signature_analysis">Signatu=
re analysis</h4><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsectio=
n-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3D=
Linear-feedback_shift_register&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D15" title=3D=
"Edit section: Signature analysis"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-e=
ditsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>In <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Built-in_self-test" title=3D=
"Built-in self-test">built-in self-test</a> (BIST) techniques, storing all =
the circuit outputs on chip is not possible, but the circuit output can be =
compressed to form a signature that will later be compared to the golden si=
gnature (of the good circuit) to detect faults. Since this compression is l=
ossy, there is always a possibility that a faulty output also generates the=
 same signature as the golden signature and the faults cannot be detected. =
This condition is called error masking or aliasing. BIST is accomplished wi=
th a multiple-input signature register (MISR or MSR), which is a type of LF=
SR. A standard LFSR has a single XOR or XNOR gate, where the input of the g=
ate is connected to several "taps" and the output is connected to the input=
 of the first flip-flop. A MISR has the same structure, but the input to ev=
ery flip-flop is fed through an XOR/XNOR gate. For example, a 4-bit MISR ha=
s a 4-bit parallel output and a 4-bit parallel input. The input of the firs=
t flip-flop is XOR/XNORd with parallel input bit zero and the "taps". Every=
 other flip-flop input is XOR/XNORd with the preceding flip-flop output and=
 the corresponding parallel input bit. Consequently, the next state of the =
MISR depends on the last several states opposed to just the current state. =
Therefore, a MISR will always generate the same golden signature given that=
 the input sequence is the same every time.
Recent applications<sup id=3D"cite_ref-17" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear-feedback_shift_register#cite_note-17"><=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>17<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span>=
</a></sup> are proposing set-reset flip-flops as "taps" of the LFSR. This a=
llows the BIST system to optimise storage, since set-reset flip-flops can s=
ave the initial seed to generate the whole stream of bits from the LFSR. Ne=
vertheless, this requires changes in the architecture of BIST, is an option=
 for specific applications.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Uses_in_digital_broadcastin=
g_and_communications">Uses in digital broadcasting and communications</h3><=
span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</spa=
n><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DLinear-feedback_s=
hift_register&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D16" title=3D"Edit section: Us=
es in digital broadcasting and communications"><span>edit</span></a><span c=
lass=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Scrambling">Scrambling</h4>=
<span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</sp=
an><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DLinear-feedback_=
shift_register&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D17" title=3D"Edit section: S=
crambling"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</s=
pan></span></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main article: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scrambler" title=3D"=
Scrambler">Scrambler</a></div>
<p>To prevent short repeating sequences (e.g., runs of 0s or 1s) from formi=
ng spectral lines that may complicate symbol tracking at the
receiver or interfere with other transmissions, the data bit sequence is co=
mbined with the output of a linear-feedback register before modulation and =
transmission. This scrambling is removed at the receiver after demodulation=
. When the LFSR runs at the same <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B=
it_rate" title=3D"Bit rate">bit rate</a> as the transmitted symbol stream, =
this technique is referred to as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S=
crambler" title=3D"Scrambler">scrambling</a>. When the LFSR runs considerab=
ly faster than the symbol stream, the LFSR-generated bit sequence is called=
 <i>chipping code</i>. The chipping code is combined with the data using <a=
 href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exclusive_or" title=3D"Exclusive or"=
>exclusive or</a> before transmitting using <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Binary_phase-shift_keying" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Binary =
phase-shift keying">binary phase-shift keying</a> or a similar modulation m=
ethod. The resulting signal has a higher bandwidth than the data, and there=
fore this is a method of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spread-sp=
ectrum" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Spread-spectrum">spread-spectrum</a>=
 communication. When used only for the spread-spectrum property, this techn=
ique is called <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Direct-sequence_spr=
ead_spectrum" title=3D"Direct-sequence spread spectrum">direct-sequence spr=
ead spectrum</a>; when used to distinguish several signals transmitted in t=
he same channel at the same time and frequency, it is called <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Code-division_multiple_access" title=3D"Code-div=
ision multiple access">code-division multiple access</a>.
</p><p>Neither scheme should be confused with <a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Encryption" title=3D"Encryption">encryption</a> or <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Encipherment" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"E=
ncipherment">encipherment</a>; scrambling and spreading with LFSRs do <i>no=
t</i> protect the information from eavesdropping. They are instead used to =
produce equivalent streams that possess convenient engineering properties t=
o allow robust and efficient modulation and demodulation.
</p><p>Digital broadcasting systems that use linear-feedback registers:
</p>
<ul><li><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ATSC_standards" title=3D"A=
TSC standards">ATSC Standards</a> (digital TV transmission system =E2=80=93=
 North America)</li>
<li><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_Audio_Broadcasting" ti=
tle=3D"Digital Audio Broadcasting">DAB</a> (<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Digital_Audio_Broadcasting" title=3D"Digital Audio Broadcasting">=
Digital Audio Broadcasting</a> system =E2=80=93 for radio)</li>
<li><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DVB-T" title=3D"DVB-T">DVB-T</=
a> (digital TV transmission system =E2=80=93 Europe, Australia, parts of As=
ia)</li>
<li><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NICAM" title=3D"NICAM">NICAM</=
a> (digital audio system for television)</li></ul>
<p>Other digital communications systems using LFSRs:
</p>
<ul><li>Intelsat business service (IBS)</li>
<li>Intermediate data rate (IDR)</li>
<li><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HDMI" title=3D"HDMI">HDMI</a> =
2.0</li>
<li><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serial_digital_interface" titl=
e=3D"Serial digital interface">SDI</a> (Serial Digital Interface transmissi=
on)</li>
<li>Data transfer over <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_swit=
ched_telephone_network" title=3D"Public switched telephone network">PSTN</a=
> (according to the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ITU-T" title=
=3D"ITU-T">ITU-T</a> V-series recommendations)</li>
<li><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Code-division_multiple_access"=
 title=3D"Code-division multiple access">CDMA</a> (Code Division Multiple A=
ccess) cellular telephony</li>
<li><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fast_Ethernet#100BASE-T2" titl=
e=3D"Fast Ethernet">100BASE-T2 "fast" Ethernet</a> scrambles bits using an =
LFSR</li>
<li><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gigabit_Ethernet#1000BASE-T" t=
itle=3D"Gigabit Ethernet">1000BASE-T Ethernet</a>, the most common form of =
Gigabit Ethernet, scrambles bits using an LFSR</li>
<li><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PCI_Express" title=3D"PCI Expr=
ess">PCI Express</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SATA" title=3D"SATA">SATA</a><=
sup id=3D"cite_ref-18" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Linear-feedback_shift_register#cite_note-18"><span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">[</span>18<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup></li>
<li><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serial_Attached_SCSI" title=3D=
"Serial Attached SCSI">Serial Attached SCSI</a> (SAS/SPL)</li>
<li><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USB_3.0" title=3D"USB 3.0">USB=
 3.0</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IEEE_802.11a-1999" title=3D"IE=
EE 802.11a-1999">IEEE 802.11a</a> scrambles bits using an LFSR</li>
<li><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bluetooth_Low_Energy" title=3D=
"Bluetooth Low Energy">Bluetooth Low Energy</a> Link Layer is making use of=
 LFSR (referred to as whitening)</li>
<li><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satellite_navigation" title=3D=
"Satellite navigation">Satellite navigation systems</a> such as <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Positioning_System" title=3D"Global Po=
sitioning System">GPS</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GLON=
ASS" title=3D"GLONASS">GLONASS</a>. All current systems use LFSR outputs to=
 generate some or all of their ranging codes (as the chipping code for CDMA=
 or DSSS) or to modulate the carrier without data (like GPS&nbsp;L2&nbsp;CL=
 ranging code). GLONASS also uses <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Frequency-division_multiple_access" title=3D"Frequency-division multiple ac=
cess">frequency-division multiple access</a> combined with DSSS.</li></ul>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Other_uses">Other uses</h4>=
<span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</sp=
an><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DLinear-feedback_=
shift_register&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D18" title=3D"Edit section: O=
ther uses"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</s=
pan></span></div>
<p>LFSRs are also used in <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radio_ja=
mming" title=3D"Radio jamming">radio jamming</a> systems to generate pseudo=
-random noise to raise the noise floor of a target communication system.
</p><p>The German time signal <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DCF7=
7" title=3D"DCF77">DCF77</a>, in addition to amplitude keying, employs <a h=
ref=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phase-shift_keying" title=3D"Phase-shi=
ft keying">phase-shift keying</a> driven by a 9-stage LFSR to increase the =
accuracy of received time and the robustness of the data stream in the pres=
ence of noise.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-phasemod_19-0" class=3D"reference"><a hre=
f=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear-feedback_shift_register#cite_note=
-phasemod-19"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>19<span class=3D"cite-br=
acket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"See_also">See also</h2><spa=
n class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><=
